The basic workflow of the current function tests is that each tests excecutes
a .rc file in batch mode and then compares the output against a reference 
.txt file to verify it got the same result.   Both of these files in 
the directory func_test.py.data.

Some parts of the gdb4hpc output varies either from run to run, or or more
slowly over time, so the .txt files defines some mark-up elements to make the
checking less brittle.

If the output from gdb4hpc changes or you create a new test, a new .txt
reference file can be created by adding some mark-up to the .out file from
a single run.

1) Depending on how long the application takes to launch gdb4hpc will 
generate a number of lines like this:
Number of dbgsrvs connected: [1];  Timeout Counter: [0]
Number of dbgsrvs connected: [1];  Timeout Counter: [1]
Number of dbgsrvs connected: [3];  Timeout Counter: [0]

The intermediate results in the .txt file should be removed, the comparison
is smart enough to ignore lines with fewer connections.

2) Some output contains the full path to the source file, the of the path
up to the test directly should be replace with %testdir

i.e. the part up the directory up to "function"

3) Some portions of the output can be created in arbirary order.  These can
marked out with this structure:
%<
... lines from one application
%-
... lines from the other application
%>

The most common case here is just hitting a breakpoint.   Any output printed 
by the application have the same property.

4) %'s can be used on any line to delimit a regular expression, for example:
Copyright Cray %[-.0-9 ]+%
<$a> frame 0x%[0-9a-f]+% Main:214

5) Printed lines generated by the application can be especially flaky since
they might not be printed before gdb4hpc quits   So the final tweak is the 
.txt file can describe lines to be ignored.   This is usually paired with 
a regular expression, thusly:
%[
<$%[ab]>:.*$%
%]

which would just ignore any output lines generated by procset a or b.


